1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,675
PPO collects a batch of experience
with its current policy.

2
00:00:03,725 --> 00:00:07,687
Then it runs several epochs of
gradient ascent on that same batch.

3
00:00:07,737 --> 00:00:08,813
Here is the problem.

4
00:00:08,863 --> 00:00:13,350
After a few steps, the policy is no
longer the one that collected the data.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,125
How far can we move it before the
old data stops telling the truth?

6
00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:20,675
PPO answers with one objective,
the clipped surrogate.

7
00:00:20,725 --> 00:00:25,163
Let's read it piece by piece: the
ratio, the clip, and the min.

8
00:00:25,434 --> 00:00:27,421
Start with the probability ratio.

9
00:00:27,471 --> 00:00:30,934
For each sampled state and
action, r is the probability

10
00:00:30,984 --> 00:00:33,822
of that action under the
new policy, divided by its

11
00:00:33,872 --> 00:00:37,184
probability under the old
policy that collected the data.

12
00:00:37,234 --> 00:00:42,059
At the start of each update, the two
policies are equal, so r is exactly one.

13
00:00:42,109 --> 00:00:46,022
Multiply r by the advantage estimate,
and average over the batch.

14
00:00:46,072 --> 00:00:49,247
This is the conservative
policy iteration surrogate.

15
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:51,572
It is an importance-sampled
estimate of how

16
00:00:51,622 --> 00:00:54,309
much the new policy
improves on the old one.

17
00:00:54,359 --> 00:00:59,421
At r equal to one, its gradient is
exactly the standard policy gradient.

18
00:00:59,471 --> 00:01:03,146
But the estimate is only
reliable near r equal to one.

19
00:01:03,196 --> 00:01:05,446
And the objective is linear in r.

20
00:01:05,496 --> 00:01:09,684
With a positive advantage, it
rewards pushing r up without limit.

21
00:01:09,734 --> 00:01:11,509
Nothing in it says stop.

22
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:13,820
So PPO clips the ratio.

23
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:19,207
Clip of r keeps r inside the interval from
one minus epsilon to one plus epsilon.

24
00:01:19,257 --> 00:01:23,045
A common choice is epsilon
equal to zero point two,

25
00:01:23,095 --> 00:01:26,207
so the interval is zero point
eight to one point two.

26
00:01:26,257 --> 00:01:29,345
Outside the interval, the
clipped ratio is flat.

27
00:01:29,395 --> 00:01:31,795
A flat function has zero gradient.

28
00:01:31,845 --> 00:01:34,132
So once the ratio leaves the interval,

29
00:01:34,182 --> 00:01:37,320
the clipped term gives no
incentive to push it further.

30
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:39,745
But the clip alone is not the full story.

31
00:01:39,795 --> 00:01:44,095
PPO takes the minimum of the clipped
term and the unclipped term.

32
00:01:44,354 --> 00:01:46,029
Take a positive advantage.

33
00:01:46,079 --> 00:01:49,804
The action was better than expected,
so we want to make it more likely.

34
00:01:49,854 --> 00:01:54,516
Here is the unclipped term, r times
A, a line through the origin.

35
00:01:54,566 --> 00:01:55,891
Here is the clipped term.

36
00:01:55,941 --> 00:02:00,016
It follows the same line inside the
interval, and it is flat outside.

37
00:02:00,066 --> 00:02:02,466
The objective is the minimum of the two.

38
00:02:02,516 --> 00:02:07,266
Above one plus epsilon, the clipped
term is smaller, so the min picks it.

39
00:02:07,316 --> 00:02:11,779
The objective is flat, and this
sample contributes zero gradient.

40
00:02:11,829 --> 00:02:13,992
The good action is
already more than twenty

41
00:02:14,042 --> 00:02:17,292
percent more likely than
before, so we stop pushing.

42
00:02:17,342 --> 00:02:22,504
Below one minus epsilon, the unclipped
line is smaller, so the min keeps it.

43
00:02:22,554 --> 00:02:24,229
The slope is still positive.

44
00:02:24,279 --> 00:02:26,991
If an update made this
good action less likely,

45
00:02:27,041 --> 00:02:29,391
the gradient still pulls it back up.

46
00:02:29,658 --> 00:02:31,258
Now a negative advantage.

47
00:02:31,308 --> 00:02:35,170
The action was worse than expected,
so we want to make it less likely.

48
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:36,446
Both terms flip.

49
00:02:36,496 --> 00:02:40,083
The unclipped term, r
times A, now slopes down.

50
00:02:40,133 --> 00:02:42,683
Below one minus epsilon, the clipped term

51
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:45,558
is smaller, so the min
picks the flat part.

52
00:02:45,608 --> 00:02:47,221
The gradient is zero.

53
00:02:47,271 --> 00:02:52,020
The bad action is already more than twenty
percent less likely, so we stop pushing.

54
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:57,408
Above one plus epsilon, the unclipped
line is smaller, so the min keeps it.

55
00:02:57,458 --> 00:03:01,108
The objective keeps falling
as r grows, without a bound.

56
00:03:01,158 --> 00:03:03,733
If an update made the
bad action more likely,

57
00:03:03,783 --> 00:03:07,758
the full penalty applies, and
the gradient pushes r back down.

58
00:03:08,026 --> 00:03:09,351
Let's check with numbers.

59
00:03:09,401 --> 00:03:14,151
Epsilon is zero point two, and the
advantage is plus or minus two.

60
00:03:14,201 --> 00:03:15,038
Case one.

61
00:03:15,088 --> 00:03:18,264
Advantage plus two, ratio one point five.

62
00:03:18,314 --> 00:03:19,789
Unclipped gives three.

63
00:03:19,839 --> 00:03:23,713
Clipped gives one point two times
two, which is two point four.

64
00:03:23,763 --> 00:03:27,238
The min is the clipped value,
so the gradient is zero.

65
00:03:27,288 --> 00:03:28,089
Case two.

66
00:03:28,139 --> 00:03:31,539
Advantage plus two, ratio zero point six.

67
00:03:31,589 --> 00:03:35,526
Unclipped gives one point two,
clipped gives one point six.

68
00:03:35,576 --> 00:03:39,201
The min is the unclipped
value, so the gradient flows.

69
00:03:39,251 --> 00:03:40,226
Case three.

70
00:03:40,276 --> 00:03:43,576
Advantage minus two, ratio one point five.

71
00:03:43,626 --> 00:03:47,763
Unclipped gives minus three,
clipped gives minus two point four.

72
00:03:47,813 --> 00:03:52,439
The min is unclipped, so the gradient
flows and pushes the ratio down.

73
00:03:52,489 --> 00:03:53,426
Case four.

74
00:03:53,476 --> 00:03:56,964
Advantage minus two, ratio zero point six.

75
00:03:57,014 --> 00:04:01,801
Unclipped gives minus one point two,
clipped gives minus one point six.

76
00:04:01,851 --> 00:04:05,101
The min is clipped, so
the gradient is zero.

77
00:04:05,354 --> 00:04:06,542
So what does the min do?

78
00:04:06,592 --> 00:04:11,254
It makes the objective a pessimistic
lower bound on the unclipped surrogate.

79
00:04:11,304 --> 00:04:13,717
The clip takes effect
only when the ratio has

80
00:04:13,767 --> 00:04:16,717
moved in the direction that
improves the objective.

81
00:04:16,767 --> 00:04:20,454
When the ratio moves in the direction
that makes the objective worse,

82
00:04:20,504 --> 00:04:24,404
the min keeps the unclipped term,
so that change counts in full.

83
00:04:24,454 --> 00:04:28,879
Without the min, the clipped term alone
would also be flat in those regions.

84
00:04:28,929 --> 00:04:31,654
The gradient would be zero
exactly where the policy

85
00:04:31,704 --> 00:04:34,542
moved the wrong way, and
nothing would correct it.

86
00:04:34,592 --> 00:04:35,591
One caution.

87
00:04:35,641 --> 00:04:37,816
Clipping is not a hard trust region.

88
00:04:37,866 --> 00:04:40,692
It removes the incentive
to leave the interval,

89
00:04:40,742 --> 00:04:43,491
but the ratio can still end up outside it.

90
00:04:43,746 --> 00:04:44,609
To recap.

91
00:04:44,659 --> 00:04:47,683
The ratio lets us reuse
data from the old policy.

92
00:04:47,733 --> 00:04:53,058
The clip removes the incentive to move
the ratio past one plus or minus epsilon.

93
00:04:53,108 --> 00:04:56,021
And the min makes sure the
clip only ignores changes that

94
00:04:56,071 --> 00:04:59,983
would improve the objective,
never changes that make it worse.

